        </div>
        <div class="mech-text">
          <h3 class="mech-title">When the Signal Misfires</h3>
          <p class="mech-body">During menopause, hormonal changes cause this nerve signal to misfire. Your bladder receives false "squeeze" signals, making it think it's full when it's not. Every urgent trip, every nighttime wake-up: it's the signal, not the bladder.</p>
          <div class="mech-detail">Think of it like a fire alarm going off when there's no fire. Your bladder isn't weak or damaged. It's responding to a faulty signal telling it to squeeze when it shouldn't.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mech-block">
        <div class="mech-img">
          <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1029/7170/3631/files/ChatGPT_Image_4_aug._2026_13_55_41.png?v=1785844615" alt="Clinical PTNS procedure at a urology clinic" loading="lazy" width="800" height="600">
        </div>
        <div class="mech-text">
          <h3 class="mech-title">Clinics Have Been Treating This for 20+ Years</h3>
          <p class="mech-body">Urologists use a procedure called PTNS: a thin needle at the inner ankle sends gentle electrical pulses up this nerve to calm the overactive signal. It's backed by randomized controlled trials with thousands of participants.</p>
          <div class="mech-detail"><strong>The catch:</strong> 12 weekly clinic visits. $3,000 to $5,000 out of pocket. Most insurance doesn't cover it. And most women never hear about it in the first place.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

